 "Noise prediction ε": "",
 "This is the same machinery as an LLM's front end: <0>tokenization</0> → <1>embeddings</1> → transformer layers (<2>transformer block</2>). A text encoder is just a transformer that <3>reads</3> the prompt (bidirectionally, in CLIP's case) instead of predicting the next token. The prompt is encoded <4>once</4>; the denoiser then re-reads it every step.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>Self vs cross</0>": "",
 "Your prompt is tokenized and run through a <0>transformer text encoder</0> (CLIP, sometimes T5): tokens → embeddings, e.g. <1/>.": "",
 "The image denoiser consumes them via <0>cross-attention</0>: Q from the image latent, K and V from the text embeddings.": "",
 "It is the same attention formula as in an LLM; only <0>where Q, K and V come from</0> changes.": "",
 "Self-attention: Q, K, V all from one sequence (+ causal mask in an LLM). Cross-attention: Q from sequence A (image patches), K,V from sequence B (prompt); the score matrix is rectangular and needs no causal mask.": "",
 "Encoder details vary: SD-1.5-class uses a CLIP text model with {#} token slots; SDXL-class uses two CLIP encoders; Flux-class models are reported to add a T5 encoder. Treat {#}×{#} as one concrete example.": "",
