 "Checkpoints & LoRAs": "",
 "Different MODEL / CLIP / VAE bundles (SD-1.5-class, SDXL-class, Flux-class, …). A LoRA node patches the MODEL socket with small low-rank updates, the same idea as in <0>LoRA</0>.": "",
 "ControlNet & adapters": "",
 "Extra conditioning (edges, pose, depth) wired into the sampler alongside the text CONDITIONING.": "",
 "Custom nodes": "",
 "Python classes dropped into <0/>: they declare input types and a function, and appear as new boxes. Quality and safety vary: you run third-party code.": "",
 "Caching": "",
 "ComfyUI generally re-runs only nodes whose inputs changed, so editing the prompt does not reload the checkpoint.": "",
 "Custom nodes are executable code from the internet. For an agent that edits workflows or installs nodes, this is an attack surface: pin versions and review them.": "",
 "<0>Check current docs.</0> Framework APIs change quickly; signatures here reflect the time of writing. Node names and folder layout follow ComfyUI conventions as commonly documented; check the current docs.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>VRAM maths</0>": "",
 "ComfyUI's power comes from its ecosystem: swappable checkpoints, LoRAs, ControlNets, upscalers and <0>custom nodes</0> (Python plugins).": "",
 "The binding constraint is usually <0>GPU memory</0>: weights alone ≈ parameters × bytes per parameter, plus activations and the text encoder / VAE.": "",
 "A ComfyUI workflow is a <0>DAG executed once</0>; an agent graph (LangGraph) has <1>cycles</1> and decisions made by an LLM at runtime.": "",
 "VRAM numbers here are rough weight-only estimates; real needs depend on resolution, offloading, attention optimisations and the UI version.": "",
 "You can combine them: an agent node loops and decides, and one of its tools runs a ComfyUI DAG.": "",
